{"author":{"id":"694fb8161bd8bf91df2a28db7c649e48f8c5fa47ad3ad0234607d35f0482801a"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789210733,"nonce":"1UY165FxS4HLtLtbDrt9blMU4I0=","title":"USER_VA_LIMIT writeback guard dropped native-stack-slot writebacks on Linux aarch64","message":"Fixed in commit 8979286. Record migrated from session memory 2026-09-12.\n\n\n`wlift_aot_invoke_sm_method`'s SM-poll branch ([src/capi.rs](src/capi.rs) ~L2105) was reading + writing `(*fiber).aot_active_depth` around `poll(fiber, resume_v)` without rooting `fiber` on JIT_ROOTS_STORE. `poll()` fires GC; a nursery fiber gets promoted; the post-poll write to `aot_active_depth = saved_depth` lands on freed nursery memory. Same bug class as the (already-fixed) SM branch in `call_closure_jit_or_sync` — that one roots `fiber_root_idx` + refreshes from `jit_root_at` after each iteration.\n\nFix: push fiber onto JIT_ROOTS_STORE before `poll`, refresh `fiber` after, pop. ~10 lines. Verified:\n- gpu/api: 23/30 OK + site alive across 30 reqs (was 0-4/30 OK, site died).\n- 923/923 lib + 21/21 aot_runtime + 113/113 e2e all pass.\n\n\nThe site is stable and recovers, but `/packages/@hatch:gpu/api` still surfaces `Subscript out of bounds.` for some requests (the 7/30 that previously hung). This is a Wren-level bug — the fiber aborts cleanly, the scheduler reaps it, the site continues serving. With the diagnostics commits (`7ad5c2e`, server-style VMConfig + named closure frames + improved bounds message), the error now shows the resolved index/count and the actual Wren function name in the trace.\n\nTo dig further: the bug lives in the gpu catalog's docs JSON walker (the chain that previously crashed inside GC), accessed via `Api.fetchJson_` → SM-yield dispatch. Walking the new stack-trace output from a fresh repro should pinpoint the source line.\n\n\n`hatch/site` AOT binary, `WLIFT_AOT_GC=1` (default): `/packages/@hatch:gpu/api` returns 000 (curl timeout) on the 1st–4th request, then the site process exits silently. `WLIFT_AOT_GC=0`: 30/30 OK, RSS grows to ~400 MB. Other endpoints (`/`, `/healthz`, `/packages/@hatch:json/api`, `/packages/@hatch:fmt/api`) are clean under both modes.\n\n\nGC crash. lldb captured a deterministic backtrace inside `update_old_gen_pointers_inline` rehashing a Map key during a minor GC fired from `string::plus`:\n\n```\nframe #0: siphash::write + 52         ; ldr w10, [x1]  with x1 = 0xFFFFFFFF\nframe #1: hash_one::\u003c\u0026MapKey\u003e\nframe #2: update_old_gen_pointers_inline + 1152   (gc.rs:2045 map.entries.insert)\nframe #3: collect_minor + 392\nframe #4: VM::collect_garbage + 7220\nframe #5: finish_alloc + 268\nframe #6: string::plus + 400          ; the string concat that triggered finish_alloc\nframe #7: dispatch_method\nframe #8: wren_num_add\nframe #9: wlift_aot_mod_1__method_6_20 + 2256\nframe #10: wlift_aot_mod_1__method_6_9 + 68812\nframe #11: wlift_aot_invoke_sm_method + 2916       ; \u003c-- state-machine dispatch\nframe #12-19: SM-chain                              ; alternating mod_1__method_6_8 / mod_1__method_6_9 / wlift_aot_invoke_sm_method\n```\n\nRegisters at crash: `x1=0xFFFFFFFF` (the bytes-pointer in `siphash::write(self, msg, len)`); `x2=0x0000002B00000000` (length, suspiciously upper-32-shifted). A Map key — a `MapKey(Value::String(*ObjString))` — has an ObjString whose Rust `String` field's heap ptr is u32::MAX. The Map's `drain().collect()` succeeded, then on `map.entries.insert(MapKey::new(k), v)` the Hash impl dereferences the String's bytes ptr and segfaults.\n\n\n- Only `/api/@hatch:gpu` reproduces. gpu has a multi-hundred-member docs JSON (~20s walk on cold interpreter per memory) that goes through the fetch-via-spawned-Fiber + drive-loop pattern in `Api.fetchJson_` (lib/api.wren:131). The chain enters AOT-emitted SM dispatch (`wlift_aot_invoke_sm_method`) and stays in it long enough for several minor GCs to fire.\n- `/api/@hatch:json` and `/api/@hatch:fmt` exercise the same code path but with smaller docs JSON — they don't reproduce. Suggests timing / GC-fire-count dependent.\n- A \"Subscript out of bounds\" Wren-level error appears in stderr before the crash. The fiber catches it; the next /api request crashes the process.\n- The Pass-1 covered_fps fix is in HEAD (commit d8739d9) and is necessary but not sufficient — that closed the constructor-receiver class. This bug is a SECOND class: something reachable from the SM dispatch path holds a Map whose key, OR a String value that's a key, is missing GC visibility.\n\n\n- Not a `c570b9a` regression — workaround was reverted in 940500a and the spec corpus passes 41/41.\n- Not the Pass-1 covered_fps bug — fixed in d8739d9; constructor body's spill slot is now in the root set.\n- Not stale plugin ABI — sqlite/audio/gpu/image/window plugins all rebuilt against current runtime; physics is the only stale dylib but isn't used by the site.\n- Not request-rate dependent (originally suspected scheduler serialization) — sequential vs spaced both reproduce; faster rate has FEWER hangs because curl times out before more GCs fire, not because scheduler is freer.\n- Not a write-barrier-missed bug — `WLIFT_VALIDATE_BARRIERS=1` is silent before the crash; `wren_write_barrier` per-call stale-source check stays quiet.\n- Not a stack-map gap the validator catches — `WLIFT_VALIDATE_STACKMAP=1` reports 13 \"candidate gaps\" (informational, with \"may be benign\" disclaimer) but no hard panics.\n\n\n\n**Attempted:** Added per-Map tracing in `update_pointers_in_object_inline`'s ObjType::Map arm — log each Map's address, key count, source addr, FORWARDED state, both source and destination String byte layouts, plus PRE/POST update_value_inline + INSERTED markers.\n\n**Findings:**\n- Every Map iterated successfully traces through `update_value_inline` → updated key from SRC nursery addr to DST old-gen addr → INSERTED into the empty drained Map → next entry. Hundreds of Maps walked, all clean.\n- Every SRC ObjString (in nursery, FORWARDED) has a Default-`String::new()` value field — pattern `[00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 | 01 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 | 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00]` (Vec layout on this Rust: `{cap=0, ptr=1, len=0}`). That matches `String::default()` byte-for-byte; `forget_heap_on_source` is working correctly.\n- Every DST ObjString (the freshly promoted old-gen copy) has a valid Vec layout: real heap ptr, matching len, matching or larger cap.\n- The crashing Map is **never one of the traced Maps**. Under the trace (which adds significant slowdown + stderr flushing), the crash either doesn't reproduce, OR the offending Map's print isn't reaching stderr before SIGSEGV (despite explicit `stderr.flush().ok()` after each line).\n- Trace itself dodges the crash: site stays alive 10/10 requests with tracing; 1-4/10 without.\n\n**Interpretation:**\n- The bug is timing-sensitive. Adding ANY observable delay between minor-GC steps changes the failure rate dramatically.\n- The crash backtrace's `update_old_gen_pointers_inline + 1152` may be misleading after compiler inlining — the actual call to `hash_one::\u003c\u0026MapKey\u003e` could be inside an unrelated path (e.g., the `Vec\u003c(MapKey, Value)\u003e::collect()` step's internal reallocation, or a HashMap rehash on a sibling op).\n- The crash address pattern (x1 = 0xFFFFFFFF or 0x0, varying per run) suggests reading from freed/random memory — consistent with a use-after-free of an ObjString backing buffer, not a stale-pointer-to-wrong-type issue.\n\n**Two leading hypotheses remaining:**\n1. **Use-after-free in promote_typed's ownership transfer for nested heap fields.** After `ptr::read` bit-copies the ObjString and `forget_heap_on_source` mem::takes the source's value, the destination's value field owns the original heap buffer. If a SECOND `promote_typed` for the SAME nursery address runs (which shouldn't happen but the trace earlier showed Range and String both at `0x472000000`), the destination's String could lose ownership. The duplicate-address observation came from BEFORE the Pass-1 fix and may not still apply; needs re-verification with the current binary.\n2. **Hidden HashMap re-entry during update_pointers_in_object_inline.** The `Vec::collect()` over drained entries allocates. Could that alloc trigger Rust-internal hashing of some unrelated thread-local that aliases the GC's view? Speculative.\n\n\n1. Disassemble `update_old_gen_pointers_inline` from the release binary (no trace) and identify what's at the EXACT offset reported by the crash — confirm it really is the Map.entries.insert call site vs an inlined collect/reserve.\n2. Try `WLIFT_GC_NURSERY_MB=1` (tiny nursery → very frequent minor GCs) on the gpu-api repro — should accelerate the crash and narrow which alloc triggers it.\n3. Add a more lightweight trace that just records each Map address + a SHA of its key bytes into a ring buffer in memory (no eprintln), dumped only after a SIGSEGV handler. This avoids the timing perturbation that masks the bug.\n4. Try selectively disabling SM-yield method lowering for the gpu /api path to see if the bug requires SM dispatch specifically.\n5. Cross-check against the Rust standard library version — `String::default()` byte layout differing from what `forget_heap_on_source` assumes would be a fundamental issue.\n\n\n**Bisect via env vars at AOT compile time:**\n- `WLIFT_USE_KRIO_FIBERS=1` (disables SM tainting entirely → empty taint set): 0/30 OK, **30/30 hang, site ALIVE through all 30 reqs.** Confirms crash IS in the SM path.\n- `WLIFT_AOT_NO_CALL_N=1` (keeps yield-method SM but drops `call(_)` taint propagation): same result — 30/30 hang, site alive. Confirms the bug specifically requires the `call(N)` SM expansion.\n\n**Conclusion:** `/api/@hatch:gpu` crashes are in the SM-yield + `call(N)` cross-fn-call dispatch path. Disabling SM avoids the crash but breaks server functionality (request fibers never make progress; documented in [[project-aot-yield-architecture]]).\n\n**Attempted fix (reverted — introduces regressions):** `wlift_aot_invoke_sm_method` has two fallback branches reading args from `aot_frames.last()`:\n- The native/foreign-method fallback at capi.rs:~1944 (when the SM resolver finds a `Method::Native`).\n- The non-SM-callee fallback at capi.rs:~2022 (when the resolved closure isn't SM-tainted).\n\nBoth should use `aot_active_depth + 1` instead of `.last()` — same bug class as the fix in commit 0611a6d (`wlift_aot_sm_load_arg`), missed in these two spots. Changing both to:\n\n```rust\nlet callee_idx = (*fiber).aot_active_depth + 1;\nlet v = frames.get(callee_idx).or_else(|| frames.last())\n    .and_then(|f| f.saved_values.get(i).copied())\n    .unwrap_or_else(crate::runtime::value::Value::null);\n```\n\n**Improves gpu/api from 0/30 → 23/30 OK with site alive across 30 reqs** but **regresses hatch-http spec from 20/25 → 9/25 pass**. The active_depth+1 logic isn't fully correct for non-resume initial-entry cases — needs a more careful analysis of when `active_depth` is stale vs current.\n\n**Definitive next direction:** the bug IS in invoke_sm_method's arg loading. Need to instrument `aot_active_depth` vs `aot_frames.len()` at every entry point of invoke_sm_method to derive the right invariant for each branch. Likely the rule is:\n- On initial-entry (no resume): aot_frames.last() == aot_frames[active_depth + 1] (single new frame on top).\n- On resume (deep chain): aot_frames.last() != aot_frames[active_depth + 1] — the deep leaf is on top, but the parent's direct callee is at active_depth+1.\n\n`active_depth` itself may not get advanced at the right point — that's worth re-auditing in `vm_interp::handle_jit_fiber_action` / the SM poll dispatcher.\n\nMemory: leaving this for the next session. Investigation transferable; the env-var bisect (krio + no-call-n) is the cleanest reproducer.\n\n\n**Audit code:** Added `WLIFT_SM_INVOKE_AUDIT=1` telemetry at every entry to `wlift_aot_invoke_sm_method` logging `aot_active_depth`, `aot_frames.len()`, computed `callee_idx = active_depth + 1`, `last_idx = frames_len - 1`, and `same = (callee_idx == last_idx)`. Also dumped first 3 slot values from BOTH frames at the native-fallback and non-SM-callee branch sites.\n\n**Findings during gpu/api crash repro (3 reqs, crash on req 3, ~32k SM-INVOKE entries):**\n- `callee_idx == last_idx` at EVERY entry. `same=true` on every line except a handful of `refreshLoop()` cases where divergence existed BUT those calls went through the SM-poll branch (not the fallback branches).\n- At all 20 actual fallback-branch hits (non-sm-callee), `last == callee_idx`. The branch's `.last()` read is reading from the EXACT same frame the proposed `active_depth+1` fix would.\n- The previous \"fix\" (changing `.last()` → `active_depth + 1`) couldn't have functionally changed anything at the fallback sites — same frame, same slot, same value. Whatever caused gpu/api 0→23 OK and http 20→9 pass must have been **indirect** (compiler reordering, timing change in race conditions, or a subtle aliasing effect from reading `active_depth` once vs `aot_frames.last()` per-iter).\n\n**Conclusion:** **The frame-indexing hypothesis is WRONG.** invoke_sm_method's arg loading is already reading from the correct frame. The bug is somewhere else in the SM-yield + GC interaction.\n\n**Remaining hypotheses:**\n1. `aot_frames[].saved_values` Vec re-grow during a deep chain reallocates the backing buffer, but some captured `*mut Value` cache from a prior step now dangles. Worth instrumenting Vec's capacity changes.\n2. `ObjString` interning across SM frames — when an SM-saved key's String content matches an interned one, the intern table de-dup may swap pointers under the GC's feet. The intern table IS scanned (update_intern_table_inline), but the order of operations between drain/insert and intern updates could expose a window.\n3. promote_typed for an Instance whose `fields` ptr is itself in nursery but FORWARDED later than the Instance itself — does the order of nursery_objects iteration guarantee fields-after-instance promotion? If fields gets walked first, instance's fields ptr could be updated to point at... nothing useful?\n4. `wren_aot_check_error` at every block entry — if an SM resume races with a GC mid-block, the saved_values for the resume block could read pre-GC values.\n\n**Next directions for a future session:**\n- Move investigation focus AWAY from `wlift_aot_invoke_sm_method`'s arg-loading (confirmed clean).\n- Instrument `process_nursery` to log every promote with source/dest addresses + obj_type, looking for two distinct objects sharing a nursery address across consecutive GCs (re-use without proper reset).\n- Look at the `saved_values` Vec lifetime: when does the backing buffer get reallocated? Could a saved `\u0026[Value]` pointer survive a Vec re-grow without re-fetch?\n\n\n**Tool built:** `src/runtime/gc_ringbuf.rs` — async-signal-safe mmap-backed ring buffer for capturing GC events. Survives SIGSEGV (file persists across process death). Enable with `WLIFT_GC_RINGBUF=\u003cpath\u003e`. Post-mortem dumper at `examples/dump_gc_ringbuf.rs`. Zero cost when disabled (gated on a single atomic load). Records: GcBegin/End, PromoteBegin/End, Drop, MapDrainBegin/End, MapKeyForward/Insert. Ring size 4096 events × 64 bytes = 256 KB. Lock-free single-writer.\n\n**Captured the exact corruption mechanism:**\n\n```\n[1341232] MapKeyForward 0xb5163fc60 k_before=0xfffc00062d085c40 k_after=0xfffc000b579336b8  ✓ valid (nursery→old)\n[1341234] MapKeyForward 0xb5163fc60 k_before=0xfffc00062ef413a8 k_after=0xfffc000bffffffff  ✗ CORRUPT\n```\n\nThe last MapKeyForward before the crash shows `update_value_inline` returning `0xfffc000bffffffff` as the new key Value. That value is `TAG_OBJ | 0x000bffffffff` — and the lower 48 bits `0xbffffffff` ARE NOT a valid old-gen pointer (valid old-gen pointers look like `0xb5xxxxxxxx`). The hash on this bogus key is exactly the SIGSEGV we've been chasing.\n\n**The corruption is in `(*nursery_header).next`:**\n\n`update_value_inline`'s logic: for a forwarded nursery key, read `(*header).next` as the new old-gen address. For most keys this returns a valid arena pointer (set by `process_nursery` at `(*obj).next = new_ptr` line gc.rs:1383). For the corrupt one, it returns bits matching the NaN-boxed `TAG_OBJ` pattern — `0xfffc000bffffffff`.\n\nThe `next` field of a nursery `ObjHeader` at offset 8 should ALWAYS be a raw pointer. Finding NaN-boxed Value bits there means the bytes at offset 8 of that header were overwritten by code storing a Wren `Value`. Possibilities:\n- The Map's key isn't actually pointing at a real ObjHeader — it's a stale address whose bytes happen to look like a FORWARDED header (gc_mark byte = 3 at offset 1) with the `next` slot containing whatever data was there.\n- A `Value::object(p)` got written into a header's offset-8 slot via type confusion (e.g., a `*mut u64` was used where a `*mut ObjHeader` should have been, and a write to slot 1 of that ptr hit offset 8).\n\n**Instrumentation perturbs:** With the ringbuf enabled (zero-overhead when disabled, but the `record` calls add ~3 atomic ops per call when enabled), the original crash signature shifted from `update_old_gen_pointers_inline+1152` (hash crash on bogus key) to `dispatch_call+1224` (different post-GC failure). This suggests MULTIPLE concurrent bug manifestations in the SM-yield + GC interaction — fixing one exposes the next.\n\n**Tree state (not committed):** infrastructure preserved for future sessions. Five files: `src/runtime/gc_ringbuf.rs` (new), `examples/dump_gc_ringbuf.rs` (new), instrumentation in `src/runtime/gc.rs` (records at MapDrain, PromoteBegin/End, Drop, GcBegin/End), `src/runtime/mod.rs` (mod decl), `src/runtime/vm.rs` (calls `init_if_enabled` at VM::new). All gated on env var; verified zero regression with WLIFT_GC_RINGBUF unset.\n\n\nThe corruption is in `(*nursery_header).next` reading NaN-boxed bits. To trace where it gets written:\n\n1. **Add a `record` event at `(*obj).next = new_ptr;` (gc.rs:1383)** that includes the address being written and the new_ptr value. After process_nursery completes, replay the ring to find: did the corrupt header `0x62ef413a8` get `next` set to a valid arena address, then corrupted later? Or did it never go through promote? If it never got promoted (no PromoteEnd event for it), then update_value_inline is reading bytes at an address that's NOT a real ObjHeader — confirming the \"stale slot address\" theory.\n\n2. **If the stale-slot theory holds**, the actual bug is that a Map's key got POPULATED with the wrong pointer. Look at `wren_map_set` callers — particularly anything storing a Map's key from a Wren Value-typed field that might hold a non-object Value or a stale pointer. The SM-yield path's `wlift_aot_sm_save_value` / `_load_value` might be the culprit if it stores a Value into a slot that's later interpreted as a Map key pointer.\n\n3. **Alternative angle**: enable the ringbuf, run with `WLIFT_GC_NURSERY_MB=1` to force GCs after every allocation. Will significantly increase the rate of GCs and may expose the bug faster + more reproducibly.\n\n**Audit code:** Added `WLIFT_SM_VEC_AUDIT=1` to `wlift_aot_sm_save_value` and `wlift_aot_sm_save_arg`, logging every (frame, slot, old_ptr, new_ptr, old_cap, new_cap) when the Vec resizes.\n\n**Run during gpu/api crash repro (3 reqs, crash on req 3):**\n- 12,260 Vec growth events total (10,542 from save_value, 1,718 from save_arg).\n- Growth patterns are textbook Rust amortized doubling: 4→8→16→32→64, plus explicit-slot jumps (4→45, 4→10) when codegen requests a high slot index.\n- The \"old freed\" buffer addresses get reused across frames by the system allocator's size-bucket pool — normal behavior, not aliasing.\n- The crash isn't temporally correlated with any specific realloc: events are uniformly distributed throughout each request, no spike right before the SIGSEGV.\n- 10 unique fiber addresses across the run; each fiber's growth pattern is independent.\n\n**Conclusion: hypothesis #1 (Vec realloc dangling pointer) ruled out.** No production code caches a `*mut Value` from `saved_values` across function calls (verified by grep earlier). All save/load paths read fresh from the current Vec state. Reallocs happen but don't dangle anything.\n\n**Remaining hypotheses:**\n2. **Intern table race** — when forget_heap_on_source mem::takes a String, the destination owns the buffer. The intern table holds a *mut ObjString that gets updated by update_intern_table_inline AFTER update_old_gen_pointers_inline. If a Map's drain-and-reinsert calls hash_one on a key whose String content is being interned-de-interned, race window. Worth examining the order of these phases.\n3. **process_nursery iteration order** — Instance promotion's post-fix re-allocates `fields` array in old_arena. If the iteration order of nursery_objects walks Instance BEFORE the fields buffer that lives later in the same nursery chunk, the order is fine. But what if there's an Instance with `fields_owned=false` but `fields` pointing at a *different* nursery region? Probably not, but worth a look.\n\n**Verdict / Next session approach:**\n- Hypothesis testing approach is hitting diminishing returns — three sessions, ruled out: frame-indexing, Vec-realloc.\n- **Pivot recommended:** build the SIGSEGV-handler ring-buffer logger (option #2 from the 2026-05-24 plan). Capture last 256 GC operations into a thread-local fixed-size buffer, dump on SIGSEGV. Zero perturbation. ~4-8 hr of tooling work, then targeted bisection of WHAT GC operation precedes the crash.\n- Alternative: pragmatic acceptance — `WLIFT_AOT_GC=0` is a working escape hatch, file gpu/api as a known limitation, ship the AOT GC plan as substantially-complete (41/41 spec corpus, constructor crash fixed, RSS bounded, 2-3× perf vs tiered).\n\n\nWhen debugging an AOT-mode `/api` (or similar) crash:\n1. Reproduce under `WLIFT_AOT_GC=1` then `=0`. If `=0` is clean, GC is the cause.\n2. Run under lldb (the `process launch` + `bt 20 + register read` recipe in this note). Look for `update_old_gen_pointers_inline` in the backtrace — that's the rehash crash signature.\n3. If the backtrace includes `wlift_aot_invoke_sm_method`, the SM-yield path is involved; root cause is likely a missing save/load or stack-map declaration around an SM helper.\n4. Cross-check against `--mode tiered` to confirm AOT-specific.\n\nFiled 2026-05-23 alongside the AOT GC plan closure. Phases 0–7 complete; this is the next bug.","files":null}]}